  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CO_Mozambique</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">On 18 March, the National Institute for Disaster Risk Management (INGD) indicated that Cyclone Jude has affected 390,222 ppl across6 provinces. Nampula was hit hardest, with 332,583 inds. affected, widespread destruction of 88.0000 homes, 81 health facilities, and 272 schools. This comes on top of cyclone Dekeledi &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; Chido affecting the northern part of Mozambique. Oxfam is intervening in Nampula districts that are 1st priority/most affected (UNOCHA RNA, 24/03): Ilha de Mozambique, Mossuril &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; Monapo with respectively 61276, 21636 &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; 18760 affected Inds. that remain hard-to-reach. Oxfam &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; partner KULIMA took the lead to assess the WASH &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; Protection sectors, have access &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; have started a response. The project has been designed based on multisectoralneeds assessments in close consultation with other stakeholders, finding major gaps in protection &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; WASH, for example many water sources are destroyed, polluted or inaccessible due to flooding (20% HH has no access at all) &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; 60% lack proper sanitationservices as well as hygiene items (65% HH affected) (UNOCHA). Persons with disabilities face severe barriers in accessing assistance. Oxfam &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; KULIMA will address needs by rehabilitating 30 water points, distribution of hygiene kits, constructing accessible latrines &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; rehabilitating water infrastructure for schools &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; health facilities (18 total). Most urgent protection needs include emotional distress, lack of civil documentation, exposure to violence, barriers to mobility &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; shelter, and increased risk of GBV (2nd highest security priority, UNOCHA). Specifically, women &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; girls face heightened protection risks, including domestic violence, exploitation, and abuse of power. Oxfam &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; KULIMA will e.g. set up community-based protection networks, distribute menstrual hygiene kits &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; strengthen GBV referral systems to fill the gap. Oxfam &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; KULIMA are well-poised to respondwith established WASH expertise with protection mainstreaming focused on providing long-term impact.

The responses in Mozambique aims to address the needs of people affected by the cyclones in the region. Humanitarian efforts will provide support to people in need through a multi-sectoral approach, including e.g. access to clean drinking water, hygiene kits and GBV referrals. Much of the infrastructure has been damaged, and we will e.g. support water rehabilitation for schools and health clinics.</narrative>
  </description>
